WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.090
When I was 18 years old, everything I knew
about paying for college could have fit on

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.009
the postage stamp that came on my acceptance
letter.

00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:13.039
Going to college can be a gold-mine -- the
average college graduate makes about a million

00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:16.700
dollars more over their lifetime than someone
without a degree!

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.340
But the overall profitability of your degree
depends greatly on how you pay for it.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.410
The path to hitting the mother-lode can be
strewn with twists, turns and dead ends.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:31.420
But if you’re willing to put in the work,
there’s actually a lot of gold in them thar hills!

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.420
70% of college students don’t have their
education costs fully covered by family.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.120
So prospecting for financial aid is a new
student’s most important first step.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.280
But where to begin?

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:56.460
With the ultimate financial aid treasure-map
-- the Free Application For Student Aid, or FAFSA.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.019
It’s a 108 questions that digs into
your personal and financial history.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:06.040
It’s your best shot for obtaining the most
amount of free or low-cost college aid out there.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.000
Surprisingly, only 61% of high school students
are filling it out.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.220
Yes, it might look intimidating at first.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.460
But most people report that completing the
entire form only takes about an hour, it can

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.630
be sent to 10 schools at once, and you can
do it before you even apply to college!

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:29.340
There’s even a handy website that lists
common mistakes to avoid when completing the FAFSA

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.840
And hey...now that you’re a legal adult,
you may as well get used to filling out tedious forms.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.240
Each school will send you an award-letter
with different offers.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.020
You’ll need to sift through the sediment
to find what treasure may lie beneath the surface.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.620
The real paydirt you’re hoping for
is “free money”, AKA grants and scholarships.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.450
These might come from the federal government,
the state or the school itself.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.370
The amount you’re offered can be need based
or merit based, so having a low family income

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:00.000
or high grades will help your chances.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.800
But contrary to popular belief, there is no
“income cut-off” when it comes to aid

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.950
so don’t let that stop you from prospecting.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.210
Over 13 million students receive financial
aid every year, so how can you get a leg up

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.690
in this gold rush?

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.150
First, FILE EARLY.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.480
Gift aid is first-come first-served.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.270
If you wait too long to stake a claim, you
could find the vein has been bled dry..

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.850
FAFSA forms become available the first of
October, and you’ll have to redo it every

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:32.310
year, so put an ongoing reminder in your calendar!

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.760
If you’re still a dependent, most of the
FAFSA information

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.540
will be based on your parents’ finances.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.500
If you happen to go back and forth between
guardians’ households, spending more time

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.210
with the lower-earning parent could make you
eligible for more needs-based aid.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.790
Or if one parent has children already in college,
claiming them as the custodial parent could

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.880
also end up putting more in your pocket.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.730
And finally, you can appeal the letter if
you don’t love the outcome!

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.580
Try calling their financial aid department
and explaining your situation.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.450
You can even show them award letters for colleges
that are offering you more.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.430
You might have more leverage than you think.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:13.620
Your award letter will let you know whether
you’re eligible to take part in a work-study

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.620
program!

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.990
In 2019 only 14% of students took advantage
of jobs offered through their university.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:24.390
You’ll probably only make a few thousand
dollars during the academic year, but the

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.580
perks can be sweet.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.790
Working on-campus, you won’t have to commute.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:33.099
Your hours are always scheduled around your
classes and some even allow you to do homework

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:34.370
on the job!

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.370
Most importantly, you could get a headstart
on your career by getting work experience

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.000
in your chosen field while you’re still
in school.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.550
Now, I know the idea of cobbling together
a free ride is super appealing.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.810
But it might be time for a little reality
check.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:55.450
According to a study done back in 2016, only
18.8 percent of undergraduate students received

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.530
enough gift aid to cover half the cost of
attendance.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:04.990
So unless you can make up the difference with
savings or working a ton, you might have to

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:11.020
prepare yourself to join the 70% of your fellow
graduates in taking on some student loans.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.629
And it’s important to learn that not all
of them are created equal.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:20.400
Federal student loans typically have the most
favorable terms, depending on your FAFSA,

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.930
can even be subsidized, which means the government
actually pays the interest while you’re

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.300
in school, and sometimes even afterwards.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.600
Even unsubsidized federal student loans have
advantages.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.440
You don’t have to pay while you’re still
in school.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.900
And once you’re out, some will allow you
to adjust your payment schedule based on your

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.010
income, so it never gets too onerous.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:45.800
If you end up working in certain fields, like
teaching or healthcare in underserved areas,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.060
your federal loans could be eligible for forgiveness.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.120
The last type of aid is private student loans.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:56.660
They can seem attractive at first because
you don’t have to bother with the FAFSA.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:00.710
But you will have to show credit-worthiness
and ability to pay back the loan.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:05.060
Since this is tough for young folks, you might
have to get a family member to co-sign for

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:09.420
these.The terms and conditions of these are
set by the lender, rather than the government.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.930
And interest rates are typically higher..

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:17.690
Federal loans are currently offered around
4.5% interest, while the average private fixed-rate

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:21.500
loan starts around 6.17%.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.760
That might not sound like a huge spread, but
with the average student debt load hovering

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:31.860
around 31 grand and taking 21 years to pay
off, those couple percentage points could

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.389
mean $7,000 in interest alone!

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:39.120
Some private student loans can go as high
as 14%!

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.100
I’m too afraid to do the math on that one…but
this is starting to feel

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.260
more like fool's gold to me.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.880
As you can see there are a lot of different
paths to tread when it comes to paying for college

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.080
And I think it’s a pretty good parallel
to life in general.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:58.400
Truly valuable things are usually hidden behind
a lot of hard work and sacrifice whereas easier

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.930
paths come with a ton of strings attached.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:06.000
But remember, even if you end up having to
take out loans, it’s not the end of the world.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.460
Any degree, even a 100% financed one, is better
than none at all.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.460
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.729
